#==============================================================================================
#
#   wasm - Canonical MIR into a WebAssembly module
#
#   DESCRIPTION:
#       A direct WebAssembly encoder for canonical MIR. It writes the binary
#       format itself with no assembler in between. MIR registers become wasm
#       locals; MIR slots become a frame on a shadow stack in linear memory;
#       structured MIR regions become wasm blocks one-to-one; and every
#       checked integer operation legalizes into the wasm arithmetic plus an
#       explicit test that ends in `unreachable`, because wasm arithmetic
#       wraps and MIR arithmetic traps (docs/compiler/mir.md, "Meaning is
#       fixed here"). Each legalization sequence below names the language
#       rule it implements.
#
#       Narrow integers have no wasm type of their own. An i8/i16 register is
#       an i32 local holding the value sign-extended; a u8/u16 register holds
#       it zero-extended; u32 uses all 32 bits. Every operation that could
#       leave a non-canonical bit pattern re-canonicalises its result, so a
#       later comparison or store reads the right value.
#
#       The host contract is WASI preview 1, because every wasm runtime
#       speaks it: a module runs under `wasmtime run` or in a browser shim
#       without a bespoke host. The `luce_rt_write` and `luce_rt_trap`
#       runtime services become `wasi_snapshot_preview1.fd_write` on stdout
#       and stderr through an iovec kept in
#       the first sixteen bytes of linear memory (scratch); data items pack
#       after that scratch area; the memory is exported as `memory` as WASI
#       requires. Canonical internal globals occupy aligned, zero-filled
#       linear-memory cells after immutable data; `GlobalAddress` is their
#       backend-computed offset. A program with an entry gains a `_start`
#       function that calls the entry and hands its exit status to
#       `proc_exit`. Modules with no writes, no slots, and no entry stay
#       import-free and memory-free.
#
#       A module with slots gets a shadow stack: the memory grows to sixteen
#       pages (1 MiB) and the stack pointer starts at its top, growing down
#       toward the data. Every prologue traps when the new frame would dip
#       below the end of the data area, so runaway recursion is a trap, not
#       silent corruption of data.
#
#       A MIR `Call` pushes its arguments and uses wasm's direct `call`.
#       Callable values use an artifact-local table: defined functions keep
#       their stable MIR-function slots, addressed C imports follow them, and
#       `CallIndirect` supplies its exact MIR signature to wasm's checked
#       `call_indirect`. The table exists only in
#       modules that use either instruction. MIR parameter registers
#       `r0..rN-1` are exactly wasm's parameter locals `0..N-1`, so registers
#       map onto locals without renumbering; only the remainder are declared.
#
#       `wasm_plan.luc` first settles types, index spaces, table membership,
#       and concrete memory offsets. Read `emit` top-down from that immutable
#       plan: it writes type, import, function, optional table, memory, global,
#       export, optional elements, code, and data sections in binary order.
#       Instruction encoders follow by family; float-constant and LEB128
#       helpers at the end are the only byte-level plumbing.
#
#
#       This is the Wasm dispatch point of the profile layout (plan.md §5.0):
#       runtime instructions and the full-Luce start body are encoded by
#       `profiles/full/backends/wasm_emission.luc` over the shared byte
#       vocabulary in `wasm_encoding.luc`; `wasm_plan.luc` asks
#       `wasm_planning.luc` what those instructions need.
#==============================================================================================

